<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#karmapa">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karmapa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Karmapa is the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-kagyu">Karma Kagyu</a> school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> and the oldest line of reincarnated lamas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, beginning with Düsum Khyenpa in the 12th century. The Karmapas were patrons and allies of the Rinpungpa and Tsangpa rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a> in the 15th to 17th centuries, rivals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karmapa.jpg" alt="Karmapa" width="266" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: an employee of the Austrian Buddhist society · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karmapa16_3_gross.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
